    description: "FOUND by shipping INV-pusin's fix on a branch and MEASURING THE PUBLISHED\nOUTPUT before merging it. The fix is correct in itself and the item is now\nBLOCKED ON THIS ONE, because closing INV-pusin without closing this converts a\nright-for-the-wrong-reason `inconclusive` into a FALSE `confirmed`.\n\nTHE FIXTURE, authored and already on disk at\n~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/rust_fidelity_probe_08232026/repo (zero dependencies,\nno build.rs):\n\n    pub fn exfiltrate(sock: &UdpSocket, payload: &[u8]) -> io::Result<usize> {\n        sock.send(payload)          // std::net::UdpSocket.send  -> net_send\n    }\n    pub fn drain<W: Write>(w: &mut W, payload: &[u8]) -> io::Result<()> {\n        w.write_all(payload)        // std::io::Write.write_all  -> fs_write\n    }\n    pub fn load_config(path: &str) -> io::Result<String> {\n        fs::read_to_string(path)    // CONTROL, fires violated in every run\n    }\n\nBEFORE INV-pusin's fix:  net_send and fs_write are `inconclusive`, withheld by\nthe uncatalogued-module gate over producer artifacts (`std`, `std.io`,\n`std.net`) that have nothing to do with either call.\nAFTER INV-pusin's fix:   both are bare `confirmed` -- \"No net_send chains\nfound.\" / \"No fs_write chains found.\" -- while the control still fires\n`violated`, so the analysis is demonstrably not blind. It looked, saw neither\ncall, and reported the silence as safety about a function named `exfiltrate`.\n\nTWO INDEPENDENT MECHANISMS, both measured, both required:\n\n(1) A METHOD-NAME DENYLIST SUPPRESSES THE EDGE ENTIRELY.\n    rust.py's _RUST_GENERIC_TRAIT_METHODS (77 names) blocks the instance-method\n    shape `receiver.method()` for ubiquitous names. Ten of rust.yaml's 34\n    method-kind I/O sinks are on it:\n\n      fs_write    std::io::Write.write, std::io::Write.flush\n      net_send    std::net::UdpSocket.send, std::net::TcpStream.write\n      net_recv    std::net::UdpSocket.recv, std::net::TcpStream.read\n      subprocess  std::process::Command.new/.output/.spawn/.status\n\n    Probed per name on an annotated receiver: `s.send(b)` emits NO EDGE in\n    three syntactic positions (tail expression, `.unwrap()` chain, `let _ =`),\n    while `s.send_to(...)` and even `s.zzz_unknown(...)` both emit. So the\n    denylist, not resolution difficulty, is what drops it.\n\n    THE SUBPROCESS ROW ABOVE IS NOT A LIVE GAP AND THE FIRST DRAFT OF THIS ITEM\n    SAID IT WAS. A live control refutes it: a crate calling\n    `Command::new(\"sh\").arg(..).status()` returns `violated`, 1 subprocess\n    chain, and the fs_read claim is correctly withheld naming\n    `std::process::Command.new` as an opaque launch. The denylist governs the\n    INSTANCE-METHOD path only; `Command::new` is an associated-function call\n    (`Type::method`) on a different path, and it catches the chain first. The\n    genuinely unreachable set is the SIX rows whose only real call shape is\n    `receiver.method()`.\n\n    This is a VOCABULARY CONFLICT, not a stray literal: the denylist exists for\n    precision (WI-bakak, 22 false positives from `.load()`) and the catalogue\n    exists for recall, and they disagree about ten names with no arbiter. The\n    fix is NOT to delete the denylist -- measurement 0001's band sequences\n    recall work behind precision. Resolving it properly needs the receiver TYPE,\n    which is a fidelity question (see the fidelity-provenance item): with\n    rust-analyzer the receiver is known and the catalogue can win safely;\n    without it, it cannot.\n\n(2) THE DISCLOSURE THAT SHOULD CATCH WHAT (1) DROPS IS INERT FOR RUST.\n    `w.write_all(x)` is NOT denylisted and DOES emit --\n    `rust:external:0-0:write_all:unresolved` -- but the module slot is the bare\n    `external` placeholder, so it classifies against nothing. INV-fibis's\n    untyped-receiver caveat exists for exactly this and cannot fire, because\n    `untyped_receiver_sites` requires `meta.call_construct == \"method\"` and\n    EVERY rust call edge measured carries `call_construct=None`. Rust therefore\n    reaches a clean verdict with no caveat channel of any kind.\n\nRELATION TO WI-nasuf. Same family, different language, and RUST IS NOT IN THAT\nITEM'S TABLE -- it measured java / scala / swift / python / kotlin / javascript\nonly. Rust's version is also not identical: kotlin and javascript emit NO edge\nfor ANY external instance-method call, while rust emits for most and drops a\ndenylisted ten. Filed here rather than appended there so the denylist mechanism\nis not lost inside a \"no edge emitted\" summary.\n\nWHAT THIS COSTS TODAY, stated so the priority is arguable rather than asserted:\nnothing, because the accidental gate is still standing. It costs everything the\nmoment INV-pusin lands. That is the whole reason this is filed instead of\nmerged around.\n\nNOT MEASURED: how many of the ten denylisted names actually appear as sinks in\nreal Rust corpora; whether the rust-analyzer/SCIP path resolves the receiver\nwell enough to lift the denylist safely; whether cpp has the same\ndenylist-versus-catalogue conflict; the false-positive cost of stamping\n`call_construct` on rust method calls."  # 938f
    fields:  # 938f
      statement: "A clean boundary verdict on a Rust repo must either see the catalogued sink method or disclose that it could not: no analyzer-internal method-name denylist may silently remove a call site that the language's I/O catalogue declares as a sink, and every external method call the analyzer cannot resolve must carry the call_construct stamp the untyped-receiver disclosure reads."  # 938f
      root_cause: "Two independent mechanisms, both required for the false confirm. (1) rust.py _RUST_GENERIC_TRAIT_METHODS is a 77-name denylist on the instance-method call path, added for precision (WI-bakak), that overlaps rust.yaml's declared sink methods on ten names including UdpSocket.send and io::Write.write -- two vocabularies naming the same methods with no arbiter, and the denylist silently wins. (2) The rust analyzer never stamps meta.call_construct, and verify_claims.untyped_receiver_sites requires call_construct == 'method', so INV-fibis's untyped-receiver caveat can never fire for rust; an edge that does survive the denylist (write_all) lands on the bare 'external' placeholder and classifies against nothing, with no caveat channel to disclose it."  # 938f
